    body: "# T1-2 · The same tour on a different animal\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Time box** | 1 hour |\n| **Account** | Seton US |\n| **Safety** | Read-only |\n| **Paired or solo** | Solo, with a 10-minute readout |\n| **Deliverable** | Half a page: three structural differences from PDC, and the reason for each → `04-my-work/projects/` |\n| **Builds toward** | [A · Foundational paid-search skills](../05-self-assessment/baseline.md#a--foundational-paid-search-skills) |\n\n**Do [T1-1](./t1-01-account-tour.md) first** — this project is the comparison, so it needs\nsomething to compare against.\n**Read first:** [`seton-emedco-primer.md`](../02-learning/seton-emedco-primer.md).\n\n---\n\n## The point\n\nPDC Healthcare is a tidy account. Most accounts are not, and Seton US is the clearest\nexample on the team of what \"not tidy\" looks like at scale.\n\nThe useful thing here isn't cataloguing the mess — it's working out **how an account gets\nthat way**, because it happens to every long-running account eventually, including the ones\nyou'll own. This is a lesson you'll use for the rest of your career, and it's why this is\nproject number two rather than project number twenty.\n\n---\n\n## What to do\n\n1. **Open Seton US.** Different manager account: **\"Brady US - MCC (Seton/Emed)\"**. Yes,\n   that MCC does not contain Brady US. Yes, really.\n2. **Run the T1-1 tour again** — campaigns that spent in the last 30 days, with type,\n   bidding strategy, what they're optimizing to, brand or non-brand.\n3. **Then do one extra thing.** Count two numbers:\n   - how many campaigns got impressions in the last 30 days\n   - how many campaigns exist in the account and aren't *removed*\n\n   Write both down. The gap is the interesting part.\n4. **Answer the question:** why does this account look nothing like PDC? Get to three\n   structural differences, each with a reason attached.\n\n---\n\n## What to look for\n\nSymptoms of an account that grew messy over time. **Note them — the pattern across them is\nyour third difference.**\n\n| Notice | Why it matters |\n|---|---|\n| Campaigns that **exist but haven't served** in 30 days (your step-3 gap) | A big dormant pile is the loudest sign of an account that accreted rather than got designed |\n| **More than one campaign doing the same job** | Duplication is what happens when nobody deletes — only adds |\n| **Naming that doesn't follow one convention** | Different naming = different hands, no single standard being enforced |\n| **Structure that varies campaign to campaign** | A tidy account has a template; a grown one has layers that don't match |\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Don't switch anything on\n\nThere is a lot of paused structure in here. **Never bulk-enable anything paused** — some of\nit is inert history and some of it is off for a reason that isn't visible in the campaign\nname. Reading is free; enabling is not.\n\n---\n\n## You're done when\n\n- [ ] The T1-1 four-column tour is done on Seton US as well\n- [ ] Both step-3 counts recorded — campaigns serving vs campaigns not removed — and the gap named\n- [ ] **Three structural differences from PDC, each with a reason.** \"It's bigger\" is an observation, not a reason\n- [ ] One of those reasons accounts for most of that step-3 gap\n- [ ] A line on whether a newer account could avoid ending up this way — and whether that's even possible\n- [ ] You brought your answer to the readout **before** asking Alex what it is. Being wrong here and finding out why beats being told first\n- [ ] Standard wrap — prediction first, written up the same day\n\n> **Stuck on the third difference?** Add a **start date** column, sort the non-spending\n> campaigns by it, and see how far back they go. The answer's usually in the dates."
